  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>Airalo</strong> if you want a one-off eSIM for a specific trip at the lowest per-GB price. Pick <strong>Ubigi</strong> if you'd rather set up a recurring plan for a country you visit often, or you want connectivity from an established telecom (Transatel, part of NTT Group) rather than a smaller reseller. They're built around genuinely different buying patterns, not just a price difference.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: One-Off Marketplace vs Recurring Plans</h2>
  <p>
    Airalo is a marketplace: pick a country or region, buy a fixed GB bucket (or an unlimited regional plan), use it, and buy a fresh one next time. There's no account relationship beyond that single purchase. Ubigi sells the same kind of one-off bucket too, but it also sells <strong>recurring</strong> plans that renew automatically: monthly (locked in for a 3-month minimum before you can cancel) or annual (paid upfront for 12 months, non-cancellable and non-refundable during that year). If you fly to the same country every month for work, that recurring structure can beat buying a new Airalo eSIM every single trip. If you're a one-and-done traveler, it's just an extra commitment you don't need.
  </p>
  <p>
    Airalo also sells <strong>unlimited regional eSIMs</strong> (Europe, Asia, North America, Latin America, the Caribbean, MENA, Africa, Australia), throttled to 1Mbps after 3GB of full-speed use each day. Ubigi doesn't publish an equivalent unlimited product: every Ubigi plan, one-off or recurring, is a fixed data allowance that doesn't roll over once the period ends.
  </p>

