  <h2>Is Airalo or Ubigi the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with Airalo if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You need a one-off eSIM for a single upcoming trip</li>
        <li>You want the lowest per-GB price on a global or multi-region plan</li>
        <li>You'd rather not deal with a recurring billing commitment at all</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with Ubigi if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You visit the same country often enough that a recurring monthly plan beats buying a fresh eSIM each time</li>
        <li>You'd rather buy from an established MVNO (Transatel, part of NTT Group) than a smaller reseller</li>
        <li>You already use Ubigi-connected-car data (BMW, Toyota, Jeep, and others run on the same network) and like one provider</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two Things Worth Knowing Before You Decide</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Ubigi's recurring plans lock you in more than a typical eSIM purchase.</strong> A monthly plan can't be cancelled until you've held it for at least three months, and an annual plan can't be cancelled or refunded at all during its twelve-month term. Airalo has no recurring product, so there's nothing to lock into either way: every purchase is a standalone eSIM.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Airalo's many different discount codes are personal referral codes, not one official deal.</strong> Airalo's Airmoney program gives each user their own $3-off code to share, which is why codes like NEWTOAIRALO15 (new customers) and AIRALOESIM10 (existing customers, $10+ orders) exist side by side. Ubigi runs a conventional affiliate program instead, so ADAMANDLINDS is a single code covering every plan type, one-off or recurring.
  </p>
  <p>
    For device setup and eSIM basics that apply to both providers, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide. Want the no-data-meter option instead of either? See our <a href="/holafly/">Holafly</a> page.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2>Airalo vs Ubigi FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper, Airalo or Ubigi</h3>
      <p>It depends on the destination. For a single-country US plan they're close (Ubigi is actually a touch cheaper per GB there). For global coverage, Airalo is far cheaper per GB than Ubigi.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does either have a recurring monthly plan</h3>
      <p>Ubigi does: a monthly plan that renews automatically but can't be cancelled until you've held it for at least 3 months, or an annual plan paid upfront and non-cancellable for 12 months. Airalo only sells one-off eSIMs, no recurring billing.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does unused data roll over on either provider</h3>
      <p>No, on neither. Airalo's GB buckets expire with the plan's validity window, and Ubigi doesn't carry unused data into the next billing period on any plan type.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Who actually runs Ubigi, and is Airalo similar</h3>
      <p>Ubigi is a brand of Transatel, a French MVNO enabler now part of NTT Group, which also supplies the connectivity behind connected-car systems for automakers including BMW, Toyota, and Jeep. Airalo is a dedicated eSIM marketplace without that telecom-operator pedigree, reselling capacity across many networks instead.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

